const PETROSKILLS_DELTA_12KB = {"build":"12KB","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","sha256":"820f297207e730566de15dcdf0bb426bdbb9e877c1498f5dc7732e7965b6300d","sourcePageCount":18,"printedRange":"22-50 through22-67","existingSource":"2026-07-29 16-43(2).pdf chapter/page range already indexed; byte identity to that unavailable original is not asserted.","authority":"Generic PetroSkills training, not Clear Fork operating authority","dedupScope":"Retained 12KA frontend INFO/reference records plus backend PetroSkills/OEM/process knowledge; private browser-only documents not available offline. Manual source-to-topic comparison, not a filename-only duplicate test.","addedDetails":[{"id":"PS22-12KB-01","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[1],"printedPages":["22-50"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryomode","title":"Ethane rejection at constant residue pressure","text":"The supplied ethane-rejection discussion assumes constant residue-gas pressure: it describes decreasing demethanizer pressure and increasing bottom heat to send unwanted ethane overhead. Although pressure reduction alone may suggest more condensation, the warmer demethanizer overhead supplies less cooling in the exchangers. The text says turboexpander operation is practically unchanged in this comparison. These are the textbook comparison conditions, not new Clear Fork setpoints.","noveltyBasis":"Existing records describe rejection in general; this constant-residue-pressure comparison and the warmer-overhead cooling explanation were not stated together.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-02","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[1,2],"printedPages":["22-50","22-51"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryomode","title":"Figure 22-27 is a two-expander example, not the Clear Fork arrangement","text":"Figure 22-27 illustrates two turboexpanders in series because its total pressure drop is too large for a single machine. Its maximum-recovery case is labelled 88% ethane recovery. Feed gas supplies bottom and side reboilers; the trim-reboiler heating-medium stream is shut off for recovery. In the illustrated rejection case, the bottom reboiler is bypassed, feed gas uses the side reboiler, and the trim reboiler supplies the increased process-heat requirement. The figure is a generic case: it does not add a second expander, a bypass, or an operating lineup to Clear Fork.","noveltyBasis":"The exact figure configuration, case label and case-specific reboiler participation were absent from the retained library notes.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-03","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[1],"printedPages":["22-50"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryomode","title":"Recovery/rejection comparison - source example only","text":"The source comparison uses constant residue pressure 289 psia [20 bara]. Source US-unit recovery/rejection pairs: feed pressure 1000/1000 psia; feed temperature 123/123 F; HP-separator pressure 986/986 psia; HP-separator temperature-45/-21 F; LP-separator pressure 526/526 psia; LP-separator temperature-112/-92 F; demethanizer overhead pressure 226/211 psia; overhead temperature-163/-149 F; bottoms temperature 31/135 F. The printed LP-pressure metric entries disagree (36 bara recovery versus 336 bara rejection despite 526 psia in both columns); retain that source discrepancy, not a silently converted replacement. None of these values are Clear Fork targets or limits.","noveltyBasis":"The table values and its printed LP-pressure unit inconsistency were not retained; example data are kept separate from plant calibration.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-04","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[4,5],"printedPages":["22-53","22-54"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryooperatingobjectives","title":"Product-quality basis must remain explicit","text":"The text gives two methane-quality reporting bases: methane as liquid-volume percent of the NGL product, or methane percentage relative to ethane in that NGL. Its specification-limit discussion refers to product impurity/methane content; Figure 22-28 illustrates that composition limit, not a numerical bottom-temperature limit. Bottom temperature is described as influencing methane content, but these pages provide no Clear Fork specification or target.","noveltyBasis":"Existing notes mention methane-quality control but not both distinct measurement bases; the composition-versus-temperature distinction explicitly clarifies prior wording.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-05","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[5],"printedPages":["22-54"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryooperatingobjectives","title":"Separator dump oscillation changes flash vapor as well as liquid","text":"In the source discussion, a high-pressure-drop separator level valve has large dynamic gain. A swinging valve sends liquid surges and also changes how much vapor flashes from that liquid, further disturbing demethanizer feed. This adds a vapor-feed consequence to the already retained advice to use separator inventory as a surge buffer.","noveltyBasis":"The inventory/dynamic-gain principle already exists; only the varying flashed-vapor consequence is added.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-06","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[6],"printedPages":["22-55"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryooperatingobjectives","title":"Feed-gas-heated reboilers have changing heat-source conditions","text":"The text distinguishes slow temperature-loop response in general from the harder case of reboilers heated by warm feed gas: feed-gas flow, pressure and temperature fluctuations change heat input and complicate control. It also says sophisticated PLC-based schemes use feed flow, temperatures, pressures and NGL composition, with specialized site-specific operator training. It does not specify Clear Fork tuning or PLC logic.","noveltyBasis":"Slow thermal response is already retained; variable feed-gas heat-source conditions and the stated PLC input/training scope are added.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-07","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[7],"printedPages":["22-56"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryostartup","title":"Dryout source coverage beyond the main gas path","text":"Beyond the retained three-phase dryout outline, the text states that dryout circulation must cover liquid-flow paths such as level valves as well as normal gas paths; trapped low points and dead legs can retain water. It describes keeping circulation high only within molecular-sieve capacity. These are generic coverage considerations: the source does not identify Clear Fork drains, isolation boundaries, or an authorized dryout lineup.","noveltyBasis":"Three phases, gas choice and moisture-comparison endpoint already exist; only liquid-path/dead-leg coverage and the mol-sieve capacity constraint are added.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-08","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[15,16],"printedPages":["22-64","22-65"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryo_co2_chart_12kb","title":"Figure 22-29 - select the phase region first","text":"Figure 22-29 is labelled Approximate Solid CO2 Formation Conditions. Its inset is used first to determine whether the operating point is in the methane-liquid region or vapor region. The liquid-region method uses the dashed solid-liquid equilibrium line; the vapor-region method uses the solid constant-pressure equilibrium curves. The source warns that conditions near solid formation require more specific information. This is a study-chart method, not a calculated Clear Fork safe-operating limit.","noveltyBasis":"Hydrate-versus-CO2 distinctions already exist; the figure method and region/curve selection were absent.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-09","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[16],"printedPages":["22-65"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryo_co2_chart_12kb","title":"Two source CO2 examples - not plant thresholds","text":"The text works examples at 300 psia. At-170 F it identifies the methane-liquid region and about 2.1 mol% CO2 in the liquid as the example formation boundary. At the same pressure and-150 F it identifies vapor conditions and about 1.28 mol% CO2 in the vapor. Keep phase, temperature and concentration basis with each example; do not transfer either number to a different stream, pressure or composition, or install it as an alarm.","noveltyBasis":"These two phase-qualified example values were not present; no graph digitization or new phase model is introduced.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-10","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[16],"printedPages":["22-65"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryotroubleshoot","title":"CO2 deposition can be below the top trays","text":"For the source non-refluxed demethanizer example with expander liquid fed near the top, CO2 concentrates in upper equilibrium stages. The most likely deposition point may be several trays below the top rather than at the expander outlet. Plugged trays or packing would cause high differential pressure in that tower section. The example geometry and feed configuration are not assumed to be Clear Fork.","noveltyBasis":"RisingDP and solidCO2 are already discussed; the non-refluxed/top-feed example and sectional deposition location were not retained.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-11","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[16,17],"printedPages":["22-65","22-66"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryotroubleshoot","title":"Thermosiphon return and optional injection-gas detail","text":"The source thermosiphon description returns the vapor-liquid mixture below the draw-off tray. It also describes an optional injection-gas connection at the base of the return line: where installed, a small warm, lean gas stream can provide lifting action, and rising reboiler/tower temperatures indicate circulation recovery. High-pressure residue gas is its example. This is not evidence that Clear Fork has that connection and does not authorize adding one to the model or field.","noveltyBasis":"The vapor-lock mechanism and heat interruption are already retained; the return-tray relationship and conditional gas-lift arrangement are added.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true},{"id":"PS22-12KB-12","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-43(3).pdf","pdfPages":[17],"printedPages":["22-66"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryotroubleshoot","title":"Temperature-control fault alternatives in the source","text":"Before the source manual-control test, the text lists a stuck temperature-control valve, a main-gas butterfly valve too far open or closed, and a failed controller as alternatives. For temperature that remains too low, it specifically says to rule out a vapor-locked reboiler before blaming the controller. These are source diagnostic alternatives, not verified Clear Fork faults or an instruction to change live controller mode.","noveltyBasis":"The manual-control test and vapor-lock caution already exist; the explicit valve/butterfly/controller alternatives are added.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true}],"pageReview":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedPage":"22-50","existingTargets":["proc_cryomode"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-01","PS22-12KB-02","PS22-12KB-03"],"decision":"Existing chapter/rejection principles retained; add detailed constant-residue comparison and example-only case/table."},{"pdfPage":2,"printedPage":"22-51","existingTargets":["proc_cryomode"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-02"],"decision":"Figure inspected: schematic two-expander example, not a replacement plant flow path."},{"pdfPage":3,"printedPage":"22-52","existingTargets":["proc_cryooperatingobjectives"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Quality/profit/production, compressor loading and expander pressure-drop concepts already covered."},{"pdfPage":4,"printedPage":"22-53","existingTargets":["proc_cryooperatingobjectives"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-04"],"decision":"Costs and product-quality relationship covered; clarify measurement basis and specification wording."},{"pdfPage":5,"printedPage":"22-54","existingTargets":["proc_cryooperatingobjectives"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-04","PS22-12KB-05"],"decision":"Figure composition axis inspected; existing surge-buffer guidance retained, add flash-vapor consequence."},{"pdfPage":6,"printedPage":"22-55","existingTargets":["proc_cryooperatingobjectives","proc_cryostartup"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-06"],"decision":"Three-stage startup and procedure caution covered; add heat-source fluctuation/PLC context."},{"pdfPage":7,"printedPage":"22-56","existingTargets":["proc_cryostartup"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-07"],"decision":"Dryout gas/three phases/completion check/cooldown covered; only added scope details retained."},{"pdfPage":8,"printedPage":"22-57","existingTargets":["proc_cryostartup"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Cooldown and seal-gas/oil/machine startup outline already retained; no duplicated sequence."},{"pdfPage":9,"printedPage":"22-58","existingTargets":["proc_cryostartup"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Seal-gas pressure sequencing and oil/pump roles covered by retained chapter/OEM references; no duplicated solvent or field procedure."},{"pdfPage":10,"printedPage":"22-59","existingTargets":["proc_cryostartup"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Guide-vane leakage, gradual warm startup and cold-bearing risk already covered; source remains generic."},{"pdfPage":11,"printedPage":"22-60","existingTargets":["proc_cryostartup"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Generic cold-start example values and routine operating checks already covered; not installed as settings."},{"pdfPage":12,"printedPage":"22-61","existingTargets":["proc_jtvalvedown","proc_cryoshutdownventing"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"JT40-60% example and heavier-feed/methane effect already retained; not equivalent to ClearFork operator C3 recovery."},{"pdfPage":13,"printedPage":"22-62","existingTargets":["proc_cryoshutdownventing"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Shutdown/vent hazards covered; no inferred cryogenic vent-stack installation or routing."},{"pdfPage":14,"printedPage":"22-63","existingTargets":["proc_cryotroubleshoot"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Separator-temperature symptom groups and hydrate/CO2 distinction covered."},{"pdfPage":15,"printedPage":"22-64","existingTargets":["proc_cryo_co2_chart_12kb"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-08"],"decision":"Approximate chart inspected; only method added, not a new numeric safety calculator."},{"pdfPage":16,"printedPage":"22-65","existingTargets":["proc_cryo_co2_chart_12kb","proc_cryotroubleshoot"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-08","PS22-12KB-09","PS22-12KB-10","PS22-12KB-11"],"decision":"Chart worked examples, deposition location and thermosiphon detail added; derime principles not duplicated."},{"pdfPage":17,"printedPage":"22-66","existingTargets":["proc_cryotroubleshoot"],"addedIds":["PS22-12KB-11","PS22-12KB-12"],"decision":"Vapor-lock/snowballing mechanisms already covered; only optional gas-lift and diagnostic alternatives added."},{"pdfPage":18,"printedPage":"22-67","existingTargets":["proc_cryotroubleshoot"],"addedIds":[],"decision":"Snowballing response/root-cause text already retained; no new controls or setpoints."}],"clarifications":[{"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryomode","match":"Why it matters operationally:","note":"SOURCE CLARIFICATION12KB (p 22-50): the supplied text defines rejection as minimizing ethane in NGL and sending it to residue. The older library explanation below about more ethane in bottoms is retained as disputed historical wording, not the source-backed rejection rule."},{"targetInfoKey":"proc_cryooperatingobjectives","match":"The methane content of the NGL determines product quality","note":"SOURCE CLARIFICATION12KB (pp 22-53/54): the specification limit is product methane/impurity content. The old phrase about running bottom temperature to the specification limit must not be read as a documented temperature target."}],"summary":{"sourcePagesReviewed":18,"newDetailRecords":12,"newChapterTabs":0,"newSubsections":1,"historicalSourceClarifications":2,"newControlSetpoints":0,"newGeometry":0},"policy":["Use the existing Cryogenic Process Fundamentals (PetroSkills) tab and its source-linked sections; do not create another chapter copy.","Retain prior notes and source qualifications. The source procedure caution on p22-55 applies to every startup/shutdown/derime example.","Book quantities remain source examples with original units and phase basis. Source inconsistencies are flagged, not silently recalculated.","Clear Fork/OEM material and actual piping are separate sources; no new valve moves, extra expander, relief route, alarm or calibration is inferred.","Source PDFs/photos remain outside deployment; source filename/hash/page references are retained."]};
