const PETROSKILLS_DELTA_12KC = {"build":"12KC","baseline":"12KB","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","sha256":"d54657508f949ffc4aae6dc139a61265775872f1c23f24d89961c6dcd3e576e8","sourcePageCount":23,"printedRange":"11-3 through 11-25","existingSource":"2026-07-29 16-52(2).pdf already indexed by section/page range; byte identity to that earlier unavailable original is not asserted.","authority":"Source-derived generic pump training; not installed equipment, nameplate, curve, operating limit or approved procedure","dedupScope":"Manual source-to-topic comparison against all retained 12KB reference-note records, source snapshot and exported existing PetroSkills knowledge. Browser-only uploads were not available.","addedDetails":[{"id":"PS11-12KC-01","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[3],"printedPages":["11-5"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_basics","title":"Shaft sleeve, bearing location and lubrication details","text":"The source describes a shaft sleeve of hard material, with tungsten carbide as an example, at the seal or packing contact to resist local wear or corrosion. Small process-pump bearings may be contained in the casing; larger pumps may have bearing housings at one or both shaft ends. Its illustrated oil-lubricated housing is partly filled and uses a sight glass to indicate oil level; other illustrated bearings are grease-lubricated. These are construction alternatives, not identification of the installed Clear Fork bearing arrangement or lubricant.","noveltyBasis":"The five-part description and bearing/thrust functions were present; sleeve material example, housing-location alternatives and the pictured lubrication arrangements were not retained.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-02","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[4,5],"printedPages":["11-6","11-7"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_basics","title":"Couplings: direct drive, gearbox drive and pictured types","text":"A direct drive uses a coupling between driver and pump shafts. With a gearbox, the source shows one connection from driver to gearbox input and another from gearbox output to pump. Couplings transmit torque, accommodate sudden load changes and allow the small alignment differences associated with installation and unequal thermal growth. Figure 11-7 distinguishes a flexible-disc coupling, a grid coupling whose hubs are joined by a serpentine spring, and a gear coupling whose hub teeth engage matching sleeve teeth. The chapter supplies no installed coupling model, alignment tolerance or field access instruction for Clear Fork.","noveltyBasis":"Prior compressor coupling/OEM records are separate; the pump chapter coupling arrangements and Figure 11-7 identification details were missing.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-03","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[4,6,7],"printedPages":["11-6","11-8","11-9"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_basics","title":"Horizontal, vertical, can-type and submersible arrangements","text":"The chapter contrasts a horizontal pump mounted on a firm foundation with a vertical pump arrangement that can attach to piping with support beneath it. In its submersible example, pump and motor form an enclosed integral unit placed inside a well casing and connected by an electrical cable. In a can-type arrangement the driver is at or above the liquid surface and the shaft extends down to the pump below that surface. Horizontal multistage, can-type and submersible are distinct arrangements. The source figures explain alternatives and do not prove a Clear Fork pump orientation, foundation, stage count or nozzle arrangement.","noveltyBasis":"Basic series-impeller behavior was retained; the location of driver and pump in the can/submersible illustrations and their contrast with horizontal mounting were not.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-04","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[7],"printedPages":["11-9"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_basics","title":"Multistage pressure-rise worked example","text":"The source example requires 9000 kPa (1305 psi) total pressure rise and uses 300 kPa (43.5 psi in the calculation table) per stage, giving 30 stages. The preceding prose rounds that stage pressure to 43 psi. Keep the table and prose precision distinct. This is an instructional example, not the stage count or rating of P-1630, P-1635 or any other plant pump.","noveltyBasis":"Existing records described additional impellers adding head, but did not retain this worked stage-count example.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-05","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[8],"printedPages":["11-10"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_seals_12kc","title":"Two-seal interspace pressure as a leakage indication","text":"Figure 11-13 places a pressure gauge between an inner and an outer seal. In that illustrated arrangement, leakage through the inner seal raises the pressure between seals. The text says some installations also use a pressure switch to signal an alarm or stop the pump. This is a configuration-specific training example; do not assign an alarm, shutdown, seal plan or pressure threshold to a plant pump from this figure.","noveltyBasis":"The stationary/rotating seal faces were retained; the separate two-seal pressure-monitoring arrangement and optional pressure-switch function were missing.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-06","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[8,9],"printedPages":["11-10","11-11"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_seals_12kc","title":"Two seal-oil pots: pressure transfer, bypass and inventory limit","text":"The source two-pot example uses water at the bottom of each pot: one pot contains pump discharge liquid above the water and the other contains seal oil above it. Water transmits pressure while separating the two upper liquids. Seal oil at pump-discharge pressure feeds the interseal chamber. A bypass from the pump side of the inner seal to suction reduces that side to suction pressure, so inner-seal leakage in this arrangement carries seal oil into the pump. The outer seal contains the seal oil. The chapter limits the example by finite oil inventory: a sufficiently large leak can displace all seal oil and leave pump liquid between the seals, after which outer-seal failure can release it. Its stated use on small pumps, usually below 35 kW / 50 hp, is textbook context, not a verified site rating or an instruction to add water to an installed seal pot.","noveltyBasis":"No two-pot water interface, suction bypass or inventory-exhaustion failure path was present in the retained generic pump notes.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-07","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[9,10],"printedPages":["11-11","11-12"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_seals_12kc","title":"Circulating seal oil and the Figure 11-15 balance line","text":"The source circulating system sends oil from its tank through a pump and filter arrangement into the seal chambers, then through an outlet pressure controller and cooler back to the tank. It maintains seal-oil pressure above the pressure on the pump side of the seals. The illustrated multistage pump has seals at both shaft ends and a balance piston; a balance line returns leakage to suction and holds suction pressure on the pump side of both seals. With seal oil above that pressure, leakage through an inner seal is toward the process. The text specifies a non-volatile, non-contaminating seal liquid for its application and says lubricating oil is often used. No oil grade, differential-pressure setting, installed seal-plan number or additional Clear Fork piping is inferred.","noveltyBasis":"The generic pump material did not describe the full circulating system, outlet pressure control, or the relation between balance line and both seal chambers.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-08","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[12,13],"printedPages":["11-14","11-15"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_headvspressure","title":"Ideal head equation and the 7-inch impeller example","text":"Equation 11-1 estimates ideal head as H = v^2/(2g) = rpm^2*d^2/(729*g). The table uses d in metres with g = 9.81 m/s^2 for H in metres, or d in feet with g about 32.2 ft/s^2 for H in feet. Example 11-1 uses 3500 rpm and a 17.8 cm / 7-inch impeller and reports about 54 m / 177 ft. The text immediately distinguishes the real pump: friction prevents all kinetic energy becoming head, and increasing flow increases friction losses. This ideal estimate is retained as a source example, not a substitute for an installed pump curve.","noveltyBasis":"Head/pressure and speed/diameter scaling were already retained; the ideal equation, units, worked example and adjacent friction qualification were not.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-09","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[14],"printedPages":["11-16"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_headvspressure","title":"Same-head pressure comparison: water, gasoline and propane","text":"Example 11-2 carries the preceding 54 m / 177 ft head into three illustrative fluids. Its printed results are water SG 1.0: 530 kPa / 76.6 psi; gasoline SG 0.78: 413 kPa / 59.8 psi; propane SG 0.50: 265 kPa / 38.3 psi. They illustrate the existing head-versus-pressure relationship. The densities and results are the book example, not current plant composition, a pressure setpoint or a new pump performance curve.","noveltyBasis":"The relationship and a qualitative water/propane comparison existed; the source example including gasoline and its reported values was missing.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-10","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[15],"printedPages":["11-17"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_headvspressure","title":"NPSHA equation, elevation sign and saturated-liquid example","text":"Equation 11-3 is NPSHA = A*(Ps - dPf - Pv)/SG + H. Ps is pressure on the source vessel, dPf suction-line friction loss, Pv vapor pressure at suction temperature, and H liquid-surface elevation relative to pump suction. The source gives A = 0.102 for kPa and metres and A = 2.31 for psi and feet; its pressure table explicitly uses absolute kPaA/psia for Ps and Pv. H is negative when the pump lifts liquid. For saturated liquid Ps = Pv, so the example leaves elevation minus suction-line losses as the available head. Example 11-3 uses saturated propane at 40 C / 104 F, SG 0.5, vapor pressure 1341 kPa / 194 psia and line loss 10 kPa / 1.5 psi. Preserve the printed SI result 0.56 m and FPS result 1.6 ft separately. The prose shows 2.6 m [85 ft], while the FPS calculation uses 8.5 ft. No automatic correction, conversion reconciliation or plant NPSH limit is installed from this example.","noveltyBasis":"NPSHA/NPSHR and contributing conditions were retained, but the equation/absolute-pressure units, elevation sign and worked saturated-liquid example were absent.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":["Example 11-3: prose [85 ft] versus calculation 8.5 ft; the separately rounded printed SI/FPS results are retained, not silently reconciled."]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-11","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[15,16],"printedPages":["11-17","11-18"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_headvspressure","title":"Source water-lift and booster-pump examples","text":"The water-lift continuation uses water vapor pressure 7.4 kPa at 40 C (1.1 psia at 104 F) and atmospheric pressure 100 kPa / 14.5 psia. It reports 9.5 m / 31.2 ft available head at H = 0 and, for NPSHR 2 m / 6.6 ft, an example lift of 7.5 m / 24.6 ft. It also describes high-speed water-injection examples with NPSHR 30-40 m / 100-130 ft and low-speed, low-NPSHR booster pumps supplying roughly 40-50 m / 130-160 ft head. These are specific source illustrations, not guaranteed cavitation margins, site suction limits or verified booster duty.","noveltyBasis":"The general option of adding a booster was retained; these numerical water-lift/high-speed-pump examples and their application context were not.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-12","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[16],"printedPages":["11-18"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_curvesandcontrol","title":"Pump power equation and unit-specific conversion factor","text":"Equation 11-4 gives driver power = A*q*dP/E, where E is pump efficiency expressed as a fraction. For kW, q is m^3/s, dP is kPa and A = 1.0; for hp, q is US gpm, dP is psi and A = 5.83e-4. Example 11-4 explicitly converts 40 m^3/h to 40/3600 m^3/s, uses 600 kPa differential pressure and E = 0.61, and reports 11 kW; the separately rounded FPS example uses 175 gpm and 87 psi and reports 14.5 hp. Source suction pressure is context, not the differential in that power expression. No motor rating or operating power is assigned from the example.","noveltyBasis":"Power dependence on flow, differential and efficiency existed; the explicit equation, unit factors, hourly-flow conversion and worked example did not.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-13","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[17,18,19],"printedPages":["11-19","11-20","11-21"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_curvesandcontrol","title":"Curve families, supplied pump curve and shutoff differential","text":"The source first shows a family of capacity curves for different impeller diameters in one casing at 3500 rpm (Figure 11-17), then the performance curves supplied for its selected 203 mm / 8-inch impeller (Figure 11-18). It says the selected pump must operate on or near its applicable curve and uses 650 kPa / 93.5 psi as that example pump's zero-flow differential, explicitly above suction pressure rather than an absolute discharge reading. It describes replacing an oversized impeller with one suited to the required duty as an energy-saving design choice. Do not merge the family chart with the selected-pump chart or digitize these generic images into a Clear Fork operating curve. A p11-19 prose line pairs 40 m^3/h with 250 gpm while the page's design table pairs 40 m^3/h with 175 gpm; those inconsistent labels remain source issues, not quietly repaired plant data.","noveltyBasis":"On-curve operation, throttling loss and wear were retained; the distinction between the two figure sets, the shutoff differential interpretation and specific source unit mismatch were not.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":["Page 11-19 prose pairs 40 m^3/h with 250 gpm, unlike the design table 175 gpm. The later selected-pump curves are a different source example from the earlier family curves."]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-14","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[20,21],"printedPages":["11-22","11-23"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_curvesandcontrol","title":"Efficiency curve, heat accumulation and driver sizing example","text":"For the selected example pump the source reports peak efficiency about 63%, and about 61% at design flow 40 m^3/h / 175 gpm. It explains that successive impellers can each add temperature rise, sometimes limiting how many can be installed in one case. Figure 11-18 presents differential pressure, efficiency, power and NPSHR together; the driver-power discussion reports about 12 kW / 16 hp at design flow but a 14 kW / 20 hp driver to cover the example pump's higher-flow capability. Keep this later curve-based example distinct from Equation 11-4's earlier 11 kW / 14.5 hp arithmetic. Motor sizing and minimum flow for actual equipment still require its own data.","noveltyBasis":"Low-flow efficiency loss and heating were retained; the staged temperature-rise qualification, source curve efficiency values and higher-flow driver-sizing distinction were not.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-15","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[21,22],"printedPages":["11-23","11-24"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_curvesandcontrol","title":"Example 11-5: curve reading and speed-specific NPSHR","text":"Example 11-5 states SG 0.8, design flow 40 m^3/h [176 gpm] and dP 340 kPa [49 psi]. It reports head 43 m / 141 ft. For 50 m^3/h [220 gpm] its printed answers are dP 286 kPa / 41.5 psi, power 7.7 kW / 10.4 hp, and NPSHR 2 m / 6.6 ft. The following NPSHR discussion separately describes about 3.7 m [11 ft] at design flow at 3500 rpm and 1.3 m [4.4 ft] at 1750 rpm. These are different printed exercise/curve contexts, with inconsistent figures and unit approximations relative to preceding example passages. Retain them as quoted-source example data with a verification warning, not one reconciled performance table or an installed pump limit. The source says extra elevation would be needed beyond the stated NPSHR to allow for suction friction and possible higher flow.","noveltyBasis":"General NPSH remedies existed; the exercise answers, the speed-specific NPSHR comparison and the explicit distinction from a friction allowance were not retained.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":["Example 11-5 conditions/answers do not align consistently with preceding example curves; the printed 3.7 m [11 ft] pairing is also not treated as an exact conversion. No silent reconciliation or plant calibration."]},{"id":"PS11-12KC-16","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-52(3).pdf","pdfPages":[22,23],"printedPages":["11-24","11-25"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_pump_thrust_12kc","title":"Multistage axial thrust: opposed impellers and balance piston","text":"The source depicts single-impeller thrust toward the suction end and describes its control by shaft thrust bearings. In multistage pumps the thrust contributions compound. Opposing some impellers reduces, but does not fully eliminate, net thrust; special casing passages transfer liquid from one group of stages to the next opposing group. With all impellers facing one way, the illustrated alternative puts a balance piston at the high-pressure end of the shaft: discharge pressure acts on one face, and controlled leakage around the piston returns to suction so the opposite face is near suction pressure. Piston area is selected to produce an opposing force approximately equal to impeller thrust. The chapter emphasizes close piston/casing clearance, clean liquid to avoid wear, and a design/cost tradeoff between the two arrangements. No installed thrust balance, balance-line routing or maintenance clearance is assigned to a plant pump from these illustrations.","noveltyBasis":"The backend previously named opposed impellers/balance piston/thrust bearings; the operating pressure faces, leakage return, residual thrust and casing/cleanliness details were missing.","authority":"OPERATOR_SUPPLIED_PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_REFERENCE","notPlantInstruction":true,"sourceIssues":[]}],"pageReview":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedPage":"11-3","decision":"Existing pump introduction/illustrative equipment group retained; no duplicate classification or plant equipment record.","addedDetailIds":[]},{"pdfPage":2,"printedPage":"11-4","decision":"Existing case, impeller, open/closed-vane and five-part descriptions retained; no duplicate.","addedDetailIds":[]},{"pdfPage":3,"printedPage":"11-5","decision":"Existing bearing/seal functions retained; add the shaft sleeve and bearing/lubrication arrangement detail.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-01"]},{"pdfPage":4,"printedPage":"11-6","decision":"Add the missing coupling/drive and arrangement detail; preserve existing parts explanation.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-02","PS11-12KC-03"]},{"pdfPage":5,"printedPage":"11-7","decision":"Inspect Figures 11-7 to 11-9; add type/arrangement interpretation without copying images or new plant geometry.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-02"]},{"pdfPage":6,"printedPage":"11-8","decision":"Existing multistage concept retained; add can/submersible configuration distinction from figures.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-03"]},{"pdfPage":7,"printedPage":"11-9","decision":"Add driver-location and stage-count example details; do not duplicate series-head rule.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-03","PS11-12KC-04"]},{"pdfPage":8,"printedPage":"11-10","decision":"Add two-seal pressure detection and two-pot mechanism/limits under pump sealing.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-05","PS11-12KC-06"]},{"pdfPage":9,"printedPage":"11-11","decision":"Add seal-pot pressure/leakage path and circulating-system/balance-line description.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-06","PS11-12KC-07"]},{"pdfPage":10,"printedPage":"11-12","decision":"Inspect Figure 11-15 flow directions and component arrangement; add source-only circulating-seal description.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-07"]},{"pdfPage":11,"printedPage":"11-13","decision":"Existing impeller-eye acceleration, volute/diffuser recovery and series-stage explanation retained; no duplicate.","addedDetailIds":[]},{"pdfPage":12,"printedPage":"11-14","decision":"Add Equation 11-1 and its units; keep existing head-versus-pressure concept.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-08"]},{"pdfPage":13,"printedPage":"11-15","decision":"Add ideal-head worked example and friction qualification; no duplicate affinity-law statement.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-08"]},{"pdfPage":14,"printedPage":"11-16","decision":"Existing pressure conversion and cavitation mechanism retained; add the three-fluid numerical example.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-09"]},{"pdfPage":15,"printedPage":"11-17","decision":"Add Equation 11-3, absolute-pressure table, negative-lift sign and example with its printed inconsistency.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-10","PS11-12KC-11"]},{"pdfPage":16,"printedPage":"11-18","decision":"Add water-lift/booster illustration and Equation 11-4 units/example.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-11","PS11-12KC-12"]},{"pdfPage":17,"printedPage":"11-19","decision":"Add curve-family distinction and preserve inconsistent flow-unit labels as source issue.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-13"]},{"pdfPage":18,"printedPage":"11-20","decision":"Inspect Figure 11-17(a) axes and family curves; no digitized plant curve or duplicate throttling paragraph.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-13"]},{"pdfPage":19,"printedPage":"11-21","decision":"Inspect Figure 11-17(b); add selected-curve/shutoff-differential context; retain existing wear explanation.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-13"]},{"pdfPage":20,"printedPage":"11-22","decision":"Inspect Figure 11-18(a); add stagewise heat-rise and curve efficiency example without duplicating low-flow heating principle.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-14"]},{"pdfPage":21,"printedPage":"11-23","decision":"Inspect Figure 11-18(b); add maximum-flow driver sizing and printed exercise conditions.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-14","PS11-12KC-15"]},{"pdfPage":22,"printedPage":"11-24","decision":"Add source exercise/NPSHR contexts and start of thrust explanation with qualifications.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-15","PS11-12KC-16"]},{"pdfPage":23,"printedPage":"11-25","decision":"Inspect opposed-impeller diagram; add balance-piston force/leakage/cleanliness detail.","addedDetailIds":["PS11-12KC-16"]}],"policy":["Append only missing details to existing Centrifugal Pumps (PetroSkills Ch.11) category. No duplicate chapter and no removal of pages 11-26 onward.","Keep source figures, alternatives and numerical examples distinct from plant facts and source-specific pump curves. No inference of installed seal plans, alignment limits, materials or unseen internals.","Retain printed unit/number inconsistencies as source issues; do not silently correct or reconcile them.","No geometry, pump dynamics, shaft/fan motion, control, trip, setpoint, PSV-setting or persistent user-storage change.","No new installed data plates were supplied. The prior audit and unresolved identities are preserved; collection order is a workflow decision, not evidence of completion."]};
