const PETROSKILLS_DELTA_12KD = {"build":"12KD","baseline":"12KC","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","sha256":"af1dbf72a7836c34f1de0d17aef2d8d92bc882245fab4d9b36bee11ae3764808","sourcePageCount":15,"printedRange":"13-26 through 13-40","sourceNumbering":"Pages use Section 13 numbering while figure captions use Figure 24-22 through 24-40; retain both as printed.","existingSource":"The 17-05 source/printed range is already indexed. Earlier source byte identity is not asserted; this pass compares current supplied page images to retained source notes and knowledge.","dedupScope":"Manual page-to-topic comparison against retained 12KC reference notes, source-index records and exported PetroSkills knowledge; user-browser-only files not available.","authority":"SOURCE_DERIVED_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_PLANT_DATA","addedDetails":[{"id":"PS13-12KD-01","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[2],"printedPages":["13-27"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_mechparts","title":"Cylinder construction, replaceable liner and cooling passages","text":"Printed p13-27 describes close machining of the cylinder so the piston does not scrape it. In its material examples, cylinders below 700 kPa [1015 psi as printed] are often cast iron and higher-pressure cylinders steel. The bracketed conversion is retained as a source discrepancy, not reconciled here. A liner is often inserted, particularly for corrosive gas, and can be replaced after wear or corrosion instead of replacing the whole cylinder. The page also mentions cylinder passages for cooling water. These are textbook alternatives, not verified Clear Fork cylinder material, liner fit, pressure classification or coolant passages.","gapAgainst12KC":"Existing notes identify cylinder/liner but omit the construction and replacement explanation. The printed unit pair is not a plant value.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-02","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[4],"printedPages":["13-29"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_mechparts","title":"Packing-cage and ring construction examples","text":"Figure 24-28 distinguishes a sectional packing cage with a lube-oil passage, a separate cage with grooves for water circulation, and several packing-ring cut/material combinations. Legible example labels include single-ring radial-cut laminated phenolic and double-ring step-tangent-cut reinforced nylon. A bronze example is also pictured; the intervening cut-type wording is retained as unreadable rather than assigned. These are alternative source illustrations, not installed ring materials, a part selection or a maintenance assembly sequence.","gapAgainst12KC":"Existing packing-cup/lubrication description lacked figure-specific alternative materials and cage distinctions.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-03","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[6],"printedPages":["13-31"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_lubrication","title":"Cylinder and rod-packing oil-film mechanism","text":"The source explains the lubricating film around piston rings and between rod and packing as the barrier to direct rubbing contact. Loss of that film allows packing or rings to contact metal and wear. It distinguishes the film at the cylinder wall from the film around the rod. This adds mechanism to the retained overheat/under-lubrication explanation without prescribing a plant oil grade or feed rate.","gapAgainst12KC":"Existing knowledge states maintain an oil film but omits the two contact locations and film-loss explanation.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-04","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[6],"printedPages":["13-31"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_lubrication","title":"Lubricant selection and gas-engine makeup example","text":"Printed p13-31 ties lubricant choice to gas quality and materials of construction. It names H2S, CO2, sulfur compounds and oxygen as circumstances that may call for corrosion-resistant lubricants, and gives tallow-base oil as its example for gas containing liquids that dilute lubricating oil. It separately describes some gas-engine-driven units using driver-crankcase oil as compressor-cylinder makeup, with fresh oil replenishing the driver. These are source examples, not an approved lubricant selection, oil substitution or makeup connection for Clear Fork electric-motor packages.","gapAgainst12KC":"Gas-chemistry examples and gas-engine makeup arrangement were absent; generic oil-type directions remain subordinate to applicable installed OEM records.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-05","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[8],"printedPages":["13-33"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_lubrication","title":"Direct lubrication points, pressure bias and modular divider example","text":"The source illustrates the monitoring burden of one pump per injection point: large cylinders may have two or three packing points and four to six cylinder-wall points, and its ten-cylinder example can require up to 100 pumps. Simply splitting one pump discharge can send most oil to the lowest-pressure injection point because point pressures differ. Figure 24-33 shows a six-point divider assembly and labels a bypass block for future divider additions. These source examples do not establish the installed number of pumps, injection points or available bypasses on any Clear Fork compressor.","gapAgainst12KC":"Existing notes cover divider purpose and unequal resistance, but not the source point-count example or modular six-point assembly.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-06","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[9],"printedPages":["13-34"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_lubrication","title":"Master-divider flow scaling","text":"Figure 24-35 shows a master divider feeding secondary assemblies to create 32 equal parts. The source says the master normally has a single rather than double outlet, and the supply pump or pumps must deliver 32 times the output of a single-point lubricating pump for that illustrated arrangement; Figure 24-34 depicts the special pump. The earlier two-pumps-to-one-block redundancy note is retained without re-adding it. No 32-point layout or required Clear Fork pump rate is inferred.","gapAgainst12KC":"Retained notes mention a 32-point example, not its stated supply scaling or master-outlet distinction.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-07","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[10,11],"printedPages":["13-35","13-36"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_lubrication","title":"Cooling-system figure boundaries","text":"Figures 24-36/37 identify cylinder cooling passages and a motor-driven frame-compressor closed-loop cooling-water example. The labelled Figure 24-37 parts include the water cooler/radiator, water pump, motor, gearbox, frame, compressor cylinders and oil cooler. The gearbox and coolant loop belong to that example, not to every motor-driven package. Existing Clear Fork process aftercoolers, oil coolers, shafts and connections remain governed by their vendor drawings.","gapAgainst12KC":"The retained generic cooling note lacked the diagram component boundaries and applicability qualification.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-08","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[12,13],"printedPages":["13-37","13-38"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_crosshead_12kd","title":"Crosshead motion and alignment function","text":"The crosshead and connecting rod transfer crankshaft rotation into straight-line reciprocating piston-rod motion. The piston rod connects to the crosshead, while a pin joins the connecting rod to it. The source associates non-straight rod travel with packing wear and leakage, and shows the crankshaft/connecting-rod/crosshead relationship in Figure 24-38. This is mechanism teaching, not an alignment tolerance or field repair instruction.","gapAgainst12KC":"No dedicated crosshead mechanism note existed in the retained library.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-09","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[12],"printedPages":["13-37"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_crosshead_12kd","title":"Crosshead shoes, guides, attachments and oil-system ownership","text":"Printed p13-37 describes the crosshead as a moving bearing: upper/lower shoes, usually babbitt bearing material in this example, move between guides in a bed section or distance piece. The cylinder attaches to that bed section. The piston rod screws into the crosshead and a locknut prevents unscrewing; the connecting-rod interior is bearing material and its crosshead connection uses a pin. Crosshead lubrication comes from the crankshaft/frame system, separate from cylinder lubrication. These are source construction descriptions, not installed material, torque or clearance data.","gapAgainst12KC":"Specific shoes, guides and attachment details were absent; oil-system separation was already retained and is only cross-referenced.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-10","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[13,14],"printedPages":["13-38","13-39"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_crosshead_12kd","title":"Frame construction and crankshaft support","text":"Figures 24-39 and the p13-39 text describe cast-iron or steel frame housings with reinforcing ribs and machined mounting faces for cylinders, covers and other connections. The crankshaft lies in the frame center with bearings at both ends and at each throw, positioned to maintain a straight rotation axis; the source warns that misalignment can lead to shaft failure. Frame size/power examples are generic and do not establish a Clear Fork casting, bearing count, alignment limit or dimensional model.","gapAgainst12KC":"Frame rib/mounting construction and each-throw bearing-support explanation were not in retained notes.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-11","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[14,15],"printedPages":["13-39","13-40"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_lubrication","title":"Frame-oil return, contamination removal and clean-filter baseline","text":"The source describes a frame-bottom oil reservoir, a pump commonly chain- or gear-driven from the crankshaft, and filter/cooler circulation through frame bearings and crossheads. The oil carries heat, metal particles and dirt away. It allows cylinder-lubricator makeup from the frame system while keeping the two duties distinct. It specifically says to observe gauge readings with a new filter element as the comparison baseline. Figure 24-40 separately labels cylinder lubricator pumps, the frame-oil pump, filter and cooler. The existing source 70 kPa [10 psi] filter-rise wording remains a textbook example, not a Clear Fork filter alarm/trip or automatic change instruction.","gapAgainst12KC":"Clean-element baseline, optional makeup relationship and end-view component distinction were missing; existing oil-analysis and low-pressure/high-temperature protection notes are not duplicated.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"},{"id":"PS13-12KD-12","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-05(3).pdf","pdfPages":[15],"printedPages":["13-40"],"targetInfoKey":"proc_recip_primemovers","title":"Integral-compressor speed and power examples","text":"The source describes large integral compressors of 3000 kW [4000 hp] and above as often two-cycle units at 240-360 rpm, and smaller four-cycle integral units at 600-1200 rpm. These are examples for gas-engine-integral machinery, not operating speeds or driver ratings for Clear Fork motor-driven frames. The retained integral-versus-frame distinction, fuel-consumption comparison and historical service-life discussion are not duplicated.","gapAgainst12KC":"Architecture/history already existed; the page-specific speed and power examples did not.","authority":"PETROSKILLS_GENERIC_TRAINING_NOT_INSTALLED_PACKAGE_DATA"}],"pageReview":[{"pdfPage":1,"printedPage":"13-26","existingCoverage":"Piston/ring materials, piece counts, expander spring and attachment already retained.","addedDetailIds":[],"decision":"Confirm existing coverage; no duplicate detail record."},{"pdfPage":2,"printedPage":"13-27","existingCoverage":"Rod material/coating, pressure-force and displaced-volume descriptions already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-01"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-01."},{"pdfPage":3,"printedPage":"13-28","existingCoverage":"Check-valve action, inverted orientation, chair/jackscrew and packing cups already retained.","addedDetailIds":[],"decision":"Confirm existing coverage; no duplicate detail record."},{"pdfPage":4,"printedPage":"13-29","existingCoverage":"Packing friction/cooling and clearance-pocket purpose already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-02"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-02."},{"pdfPage":5,"printedPage":"13-30","existingCoverage":"Clearance arrangements, suction-valve lifters, both-end unloading and running-unloaded behavior already retained.","addedDetailIds":[],"decision":"Confirm existing coverage; no duplicate detail record."},{"pdfPage":6,"printedPage":"13-31","existingCoverage":"Cam-drive 5-10 percent, 300-rpm example, sight-glass oil-use tracking and non-lubricated-material discussion already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-03","PS13-12KD-04"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-03, PS13-12KD-04."},{"pdfPage":7,"printedPage":"13-32","existingCoverage":"Figure 24-32 lubrication hardware and separate injection-point pump concept already retained; no new record needed.","addedDetailIds":[],"decision":"Confirm existing coverage; no duplicate detail record."},{"pdfPage":8,"printedPage":"13-33","existingCoverage":"Divider splitting and general purpose already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-05"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-05."},{"pdfPage":9,"printedPage":"13-34","existingCoverage":"Dual-pump-to-divider concept and 32-point example already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-06"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-06."},{"pdfPage":10,"printedPage":"13-35","existingCoverage":"Divider alarm/flow/maintenance benefits and generic coolant temperature range already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-07"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-07."},{"pdfPage":11,"printedPage":"13-36","existingCoverage":"Motor-driven closed-loop cooling overview already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-07"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-07."},{"pdfPage":12,"printedPage":"13-37","existingCoverage":"Separate frame/cylinder lubrication was already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-08","PS13-12KD-09"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-08, PS13-12KD-09."},{"pdfPage":13,"printedPage":"13-38","existingCoverage":"Frame-versus-integral mounting concept already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-08","PS13-12KD-10"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-08, PS13-12KD-10."},{"pdfPage":14,"printedPage":"13-39","existingCoverage":"Level/pressure/temperature checks, oil-analysis wording and low-pressure/high-temperature shutdown examples already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-10","PS13-12KD-11"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-10, PS13-12KD-11."},{"pdfPage":15,"printedPage":"13-40","existingCoverage":"Integral-versus-frame, fuel comparison and historical longevity discussion already retained.","addedDetailIds":["PS13-12KD-11","PS13-12KD-12"],"decision":"Append only PS13-12KD-11, PS13-12KD-12."}],"sourceIssues":["p13-27 prints 700 kPa [1015 psi] for its cylinder-material example; preserve the inconsistent pair rather than silently converting it or treating either as a plant rating.","Figure 24-28 bronze-ring cut-type wording is not reliably readable; no material/part is assigned from that label.","Page/figure numbering differs (printed 13-xx versus figures 24-xx); both are indexed for retrieval."],"scopeClarifications":["Book cooling-water example 38-54 C / 110-130 F is not proof that a Clear Fork compressor has cylinder water cooling or that its fan/oil controls use those values.","Book filter-differential increase about 70 kPa / 10 psi is not a new Clear Fork replacement instruction, alarm or trip. No process-state changes follow from training pages.","Claims about oil materials, zinc/aluminum, ring compatibility, valve interchangeability and integral-engine examples remain attributed to this training source; installed OEM and controlled plant records must establish site applicability."],"policy":"Append missing source-supported details only; keep original notes and later chapter pages. No installed hardware, settings, clearances, lubrication-rate or piping changes derived from textbook figures."};
