var CF_SOURCE_12NF={"build":"12NF","baseline":"12NE","date":"2026-10-25","title":"Compressor package-flow trainer / PetroSkills review","sourceClass":"RETAINED_VENDOR_TOPOLOGY + SUPPLIED_TEXTBOOK / SCHEMATIC_TEACHING_IMPLEMENTATION","notebookKey":"compressor_internals_training_12mo","ownerKeys":["c4100","c6100","c5700","jz_c4100_v_4101","jz_c4100_v_4102","jz_c4100_v_4104","jz_c4100_v_4103","jz_c4100_v_4105","jy_v_6101","jy_v_6104","jy_v_6102","jy_v_6105","jy_v_6103","jy_v_6106","jy_c6100_ic_section","kr_c5700_v_1","kr_c5700_v_2","kr_c5700_v_4","kr_c5700_v_3"],"packages":{"inlet":{"scrubbers":[{"tag":"V-4101","owner":"jz_c4100_v_4101","label":"common suction scrubber"}],"suctionBottles":[{"tag":"V-4102","owner":"jz_c4100_v_4102","label":"odd-bank suction bottle"},{"tag":"V-4104","owner":"jz_c4100_v_4104","label":"even-bank suction bottle"}],"dischargeBottles":[{"tag":"V-4103","owner":"jz_c4100_v_4103","label":"odd-bank discharge bottle"},{"tag":"V-4105","owner":"jz_c4100_v_4105","label":"even-bank discharge bottle"}],"outlet":"retained AC-4101 / common discharge boundary","definitions":["CF_SOURCE_12JZ"],"summary":"V-4101 removes entrained liquid before gas divides between V-4102 and V-4104. The odd/even cylinder banks compress in parallel in one stage. V-4103 and V-4105 damp the separate discharge pulses before the common aftercooler boundary. V-4106 is an oil catch bottle, not part of this forward process-gas path.","ownerKeys":["jz_c4100_v_4101","jz_c4100_v_4102","jz_c4100_v_4104","jz_c4100_v_4103","jz_c4100_v_4105"]},"residue":{"scrubbers":[{"tag":"V-6101","owner":"jy_v_6101","label":"stage 1 suction scrubber"},{"tag":"V-6104","owner":"jy_v_6104","label":"stage 2 suction scrubber"}],"suctionBottles":[{"tag":"V-6102","owner":"jy_v_6102","label":"stage 1 suction bottle"},{"tag":"V-6105","owner":"jy_v_6105","label":"stage 2 suction bottle"}],"dischargeBottles":[{"tag":"V-6103","owner":"jy_v_6103","label":"stage 1 discharge bottle"},{"tag":"V-6106","owner":"jy_v_6106","label":"stage 2 discharge bottle"}],"intercoolerOwner":"jy_c6100_ic_section","outlet":"retained AC-6101 / final-discharge boundary","definitions":["CF_SOURCE_12JY"],"summary":"V-6101 -> V-6102 -> first-stage cylinders -> V-6103 -> IC-6101 -> V-6104 -> V-6105 -> second-stage cylinders -> V-6106 -> final aftercooling. Compression raises gas pressure and temperature; interstage cooling removes heat and can cause condensation only when the mixture crosses its dew point. V-6104 separates resulting or incoming liquid before stage 2. V-6107 oil catch is not a process scrubber.","ownerKeys":["jy_v_6101","jy_v_6104","jy_v_6102","jy_v_6105","jy_v_6103","jy_v_6106","jy_c6100_ic_section"]},"overhead":{"scrubbers":[{"tag":"V-1","owner":"kr_c5700_v_1","label":"suction scrubber"}],"suctionBottles":[{"tag":"V-2","owner":"kr_c5700_v_2","label":"common suction bottle"}],"dischargeBottles":[{"tag":"V-4","owner":"kr_c5700_v_4","label":"1200 discharge bottle"},{"tag":"V-3","owner":"kr_c5700_v_3","label":"1300 discharge bottle"}],"outlet":"retained TP-B / downstream cooling boundary","definitions":["CF_SOURCE_12KR","CF_SOURCE_12LI"],"summary":"C-5700 V-1 suction scrubber -> one common V-2 suction bottle -> cylinders1200 and1300 in parallel. Cylinder1200 discharges to V-4; cylinder1300 to V-3; the two streams combine toward TP-B. Two cylinders do not mean two compression stages. Gas, separated liquid and the separate oil system retain distinct ownership.","ownerKeys":["kr_c5700_v_1","kr_c5700_v_2","kr_c5700_v_4","kr_c5700_v_3"]}},"policy":["One existing representative per family. No physical plant component IDs added.","All previous trainer part IDs and motions retained; earlier schematic outside connections are refined, not parallel bypasses.","Pulsation visualization is qualitative; no calculated acoustic waveform, bottle performance or fitted baffle is asserted.","Scrubber liquid and droplet illustration is not a live level, a perfect separator or a valve operation. Exact mist elements, nozzle layout and bottle internal construction remain source-qualified.","No plant controls, geometry, equations, alarms, storage or passwords change."],"newEntries":[{"id":"package.inlet","title":"Inlet trainer / retained package flow","text":"V-4101 removes entrained liquid before gas divides between V-4102 and V-4104. The odd/even cylinder banks compress in parallel in one stage. V-4103 and V-4105 damp the separate discharge pulses before the common aftercooler boundary. V-4106 is an oil catch bottle, not part of this forward process-gas path. Select Bottles & scrubbers in Equipment Internal Training. The new volumes are selectable teaching representations of these existing owners, not new equipment records.","ownerKeys":["c4100","jz_c4100_v_4101","jz_c4100_v_4102","jz_c4100_v_4104","jz_c4100_v_4103","jz_c4100_v_4105"],"authority":"RETAINED_SOURCE_ROUTE / ILLUSTRATIVE_TRAINER_GEOMETRY","sourceFile":"Existing CF_SOURCE_12JZ","pdfPages":[],"referenceKey":"compressor_internals_training_12mo"},{"id":"package.residue","title":"Residue trainer / retained package flow","text":"V-6101 -> V-6102 -> first-stage cylinders -> V-6103 -> IC-6101 -> V-6104 -> V-6105 -> second-stage cylinders -> V-6106 -> final aftercooling. Compression raises gas pressure and temperature; interstage cooling removes heat and can cause condensation only when the mixture crosses its dew point. V-6104 separates resulting or incoming liquid before stage 2. V-6107 oil catch is not a process scrubber. Select Bottles & scrubbers in Equipment Internal Training. The new volumes are selectable teaching representations of these existing owners, not new equipment records.","ownerKeys":["c6100","jy_v_6101","jy_v_6104","jy_v_6102","jy_v_6105","jy_v_6103","jy_v_6106","jy_c6100_ic_section"],"authority":"RETAINED_SOURCE_ROUTE / ILLUSTRATIVE_TRAINER_GEOMETRY","sourceFile":"Existing CF_SOURCE_12JY","pdfPages":[],"referenceKey":"compressor_internals_training_12mo"},{"id":"package.overhead","title":"Overhead trainer / retained package flow","text":"C-5700 V-1 suction scrubber -> one common V-2 suction bottle -> cylinders1200 and1300 in parallel. Cylinder1200 discharges to V-4; cylinder1300 to V-3; the two streams combine toward TP-B. Two cylinders do not mean two compression stages. Gas, separated liquid and the separate oil system retain distinct ownership. Select Bottles & scrubbers in Equipment Internal Training. The new volumes are selectable teaching representations of these existing owners, not new equipment records.","ownerKeys":["c5700","kr_c5700_v_1","kr_c5700_v_2","kr_c5700_v_4","kr_c5700_v_3"],"authority":"RETAINED_SOURCE_ROUTE / ILLUSTRATIVE_TRAINER_GEOMETRY","sourceFile":"Existing CF_SOURCE_12KR, CF_SOURCE_12LI","pdfPages":[],"referenceKey":"compressor_internals_training_12mo"}],"study":{"build":"12NF","baseline":"12NE","selection":"petro12nf","intakes":[{"filename":"2026-07-29 17-08(6).pdf","sha256":"eb8a74124d3dce13eeccb0fa438e0557287b683e92a292b96a24ec8b77bee088","definition":"CF_SOURCE_12KE","path":["petroSkills","reciprocating"],"hashField":"sha256","pages":10,"printed":"13-41 through 13-50","decision":"EXACT_DUPLICATE_REUSE"},{"filename":"2026-07-29 16-55(5).pdf","sha256":"4bbaac10c6f4b6c3b3a96797fa852ec70a4b74cd3c13ef9b99271e27157c8c72","definition":"CF_SOURCE_12KF","path":["petroSkills","pumps"],"hashField":"sha256","pages":13,"printed":"11-26 through11-37;11-36 appears twice","decision":"EXACT_DUPLICATE_REUSE"},{"filename":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sha256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","definition":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","path":[],"hashField":"sourceSHA256","pages":30,"printed":"16-25 through16-51;16-26/16-27/16-39 repeated","decision":"EXACT_DUPLICATE_REUSE"}],"referenceKeys":["proc_recip_rodreversal","proc_recip_pulsation","proc_pump_pd_detail_12kf","proc_pump_curvesandcontrol","proc_pump_vacuum_detail_12kf","ps_reflux_accumulator_12kh","ps_reboiler_types_12kh","ps_fractionation_specs_12kh","ps_condenser_control_12kh","ps_fractionation_reflux_12kh","ps_fractionation_bottoms_12kh"],"sourceQualifications":["All three uploads match retained source owners exactly. Their current detailed notes already cover the reviewed topics: zero new textbook engineering facts or reference-note identities.","Textbook plant examples, numerical product specifications, line-ups and generic startup/shutdown values are not installed Clear Fork ratings or procedures.","Existing power-factor and source-chart inconsistencies remain qualified and are not used for numerical questions.","Question objectives were compared with the prior472; source pages and explanations are attached to each new item."],"quiz":{"count":22,"total":494,"questions":[{"id":"nf-recip-01","c":"Reciprocating compressors","q":"A piston is visibly moving out and back. Does that alone establish adequate rod-load reversal?","a":"No. The combined gas and inertia forces must also change the loading of the pin/bushing during the cycle.","w":["Yes; every change in piston travel proves adequate force reversal.","Yes; a constant shaft speed eliminates the need to assess rod force.","No; the piston must stop completely between strokes."],"e":"Travel reversal and adequate force reversal are not the same test. The source explains how gas loading can oppose the normal inertial contribution and keep the rod predominantly in compression or tension. Source: PetroSkills, printed 13-46, 13-47. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"proc_recip_rodreversal","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KE","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-08(6).pdf","sourceSHA256":"eb8a74124d3dce13eeccb0fa438e0557287b683e92a292b96a24ec8b77bee088","pdfPages":[6,7],"printedPages":["13-46","13-47"],"release":"12NF","topic":"recip","learningObjective":"force-sign-not-motion-alone","s":"PetroSkills / 13-46, 13-47"},{"id":"nf-recip-03","c":"Reciprocating compressors","q":"Two compressor lines show the same steady average pressure. What can still differ because of reciprocating discharge?","a":"The size and timing of the pressure/flow pulses and their vibration effects.","w":["Their pulsation must be identical because the averages match.","A line with a bottle cannot have any remaining pulsation.","Only the liquid level can differ; pressure fluctuations cannot."],"e":"Pulsation is associated with repeated gas spurts. A steady or averaged pressure does not describe those fluctuations, and the source says damping controls pulsation rather than eliminating it. Source: PetroSkills, printed 13-42, 13-43. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"proc_recip_pulsation","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KE","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-08(6).pdf","sourceSHA256":"eb8a74124d3dce13eeccb0fa438e0557287b683e92a292b96a24ec8b77bee088","pdfPages":[2,3],"printedPages":["13-42","13-43"],"release":"12NF","topic":"recip","learningObjective":"mean-pressure-vs-pulsation","s":"PetroSkills / 13-42, 13-43"},{"id":"nf-pump-01","c":"Pumps / operating principles","q":"In the illustrated single-acting plunger pump, what makes a comparable fluid end double-acting?","a":"An inlet and outlet arrangement on the other side, so one side fills while the other discharges.","w":["Adding a second motor to the same shaft.","Using two discharge pressure gauges.","Increasing the speed until both check valves stay open."],"e":"The source distinguishes acting arrangement by where liquid is admitted and discharged during opposite strokes, not by driver count or the number of gauges. Source: PetroSkills, printed 11-34. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"proc_pump_pd_detail_12kf","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KF","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-55(5).pdf","sourceSHA256":"4bbaac10c6f4b6c3b3a96797fa852ec70a4b74cd3c13ef9b99271e27157c8c72","pdfPages":[9],"printedPages":["11-34"],"release":"12NF","topic":"pump","learningObjective":"single-vs-double-acting-fluid-end","s":"PetroSkills / 11-34"},{"id":"nf-pump-02","c":"Pumps / operating principles","q":"A small metering-pump diagram shows ball check valves. What should be concluded about a much larger reciprocating pump?","a":"Its checks may use a different construction; the source also identifies wing-guided or disk types for large pumps.","w":["Every reciprocating pump must use the identical ball check.","Large pumps have no suction check function.","Any check valve can be exchanged without reviewing its drawing."],"e":"A functional inlet/outlet check role does not establish one installed construction. The text contrasts small metering/chemical-feed ball checks with other large-pump designs. Source: PetroSkills, printed 11-34. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"proc_pump_pd_detail_12kf","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KF","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-55(5).pdf","sourceSHA256":"4bbaac10c6f4b6c3b3a96797fa852ec70a4b74cd3c13ef9b99271e27157c8c72","pdfPages":[9],"printedPages":["11-34"],"release":"12NF","topic":"pump","learningObjective":"check-design-service-dependent","s":"PetroSkills / 11-34"},{"id":"nf-pump-03","c":"Pumps / operating principles","q":"Why can prolonged low-flow operation damage a centrifugal pump even before a major loss of discharge pressure is noticed?","a":"Heat can accumulate, causing liquid vaporization and thermal expansion that can lead to impeller/casing rubbing.","w":["All driver energy disappears when product flow is reduced.","Low flow always lowers every internal temperature.","The impeller automatically becomes smaller as it warms."],"e":"The source identifies heating as a low-flow concern and separately notes that thermal expansion can cause the impeller to rub the casing. No generic minimum-flow percentage is assigned to a Clear Fork pump. Source: PetroSkills, printed 11-29. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"proc_pump_curvesandcontrol","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KF","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-55(5).pdf","sourceSHA256":"4bbaac10c6f4b6c3b3a96797fa852ec70a4b74cd3c13ef9b99271e27157c8c72","pdfPages":[4],"printedPages":["11-29"],"release":"12NF","topic":"pump","learningObjective":"low-flow-thermal-rubbing","s":"PetroSkills / 11-29"},{"id":"nf-pump-04","c":"Pumps / operating principles","q":"Why does the source discuss oil contamination when selecting a vacuum pump for wet vapor service?","a":"Process vapor or condensate can contaminate the oil, so fluid compatibility and any required treatment matter.","w":["Oil can never contact any part of a vacuum-pump system.","A vacuum indication proves that the oil is uncontaminated.","Water entering any vacuum pump is automatically suitable lubrication."],"e":"The text discusses water contamination and treatment in wet service, and distinguishes liquid-ring alternatives from oil-lubricated arrangements. It does not declare one pump suitable for every wet process. Source: PetroSkills, printed 11-37. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"proc_pump_vacuum_detail_12kf","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KF","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 16-55(5).pdf","sourceSHA256":"4bbaac10c6f4b6c3b3a96797fa852ec70a4b74cd3c13ef9b99271e27157c8c72","pdfPages":[13],"printedPages":["11-37"],"release":"12NF","topic":"pump","learningObjective":"vacuum-process-oil-contamination","s":"PetroSkills / 11-37"},{"id":"nf-ngl-01","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"Why can a reflux accumulator need useful liquid inventory during startup as well as during normal operation?","a":"It must support reflux and downstream supply through the startup transient without simply losing its working level.","w":["Its only purpose is storing vapor for emergencies.","Startup requires no liquid until product is already on specification.","Its volume has no connection to fluctuations in flow."],"e":"The source lists surge, control-smoothing and phase-separation roles, then calls out enough liquid for startup conditions. It does not provide an installed Clear Fork startup inventory. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-27, 16-28. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_reflux_accumulator_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[3,6],"printedPages":["16-27","16-28"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"startup-accumulator-inventory","s":"PetroSkills / 16-27, 16-28"},{"id":"nf-ngl-02","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"Why is a kettle-reboiler shell shown larger than the immersed tube bundle?","a":"To provide space for vapor to disengage from the boiling liquid.","w":["To store compressor lube oil around the process tubes.","To eliminate the need for heat transfer through the tubes.","To force all bottom product to leave as vapor."],"e":"The source explicitly associates the larger shell with vapor disengagement. This is a construction principle, not evidence that every Clear Fork reboiler is a kettle. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-28. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_reboiler_types_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[6],"printedPages":["16-28"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"kettle-disengagement-space","s":"PetroSkills / 16-28"},{"id":"nf-ngl-03","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"In the textbook kettle arrangement, what is the purpose of the overflow weir and separate product section?","a":"Liquid can spill from the boiling region into a product section whose level is controlled through the product outlet.","w":["The weir raises product pressure by acting like a pump.","The product valve directly controls the heating-fluid temperature.","The weir is a gas compressor unloader."],"e":"The illustration separates the boiling region from the bottom-product level-controlled section. The pictured control arrangement must not be assumed for a different installed exchanger. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-28. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_reboiler_types_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[6],"printedPages":["16-28"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"kettle-overflow-product-compartment","s":"PetroSkills / 16-28"},{"id":"nf-ngl-04","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"Why does the source discuss a weir or separate bottom nozzles in a pump-through reboiler arrangement?","a":"To keep the recirculating heating loop distinct from the net bottom-product draw.","w":["To make recirculation and product flow equal at all times.","To route heating fuel into the product pump.","To remove the need for any inventory control."],"e":"The source presents alternative arrangements that separate circulation from product removal. Circulating flow is not the same as product production rate. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-28, 16-29. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_reboiler_types_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[6,7],"printedPages":["16-28","16-29"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"pump-through-product-vs-circulation","s":"PetroSkills / 16-28, 16-29"},{"id":"nf-ngl-05","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"A thermosyphon has a density difference between its inlet liquid and outlet mixture. Why might that still fail to sustain the intended circulation?","a":"The available driving head also has to overcome losses in the piping and exchanger.","w":["Any nonzero density difference guarantees any requested flow.","A thermosyphon must have equal inlet and outlet density.","Friction affects pumps but not natural-circulation loops."],"e":"The text compares the hydrostatic driving force with circulation-loop friction. It does not establish an allowable DP or a remedy for a particular installed reboiler. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-29. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_reboiler_types_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[7],"printedPages":["16-29"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"thermosyphon-head-vs-resistance","s":"PetroSkills / 16-29"},{"id":"nf-ngl-06","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"How are component purity and component recovery different?","a":"Purity compares the component with the total product; recovery compares component in that product with component in the feed.","w":["Both are always component flow divided by total feed flow.","Recovery describes only temperature while purity describes only pressure.","A high product purity automatically proves high recovery from the feed."],"e":"The source distinguishes concentration and recovery specifications. A product can be concentrated in a component without capturing nearly all of that component from the feed. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-30, 16-31, 16-32. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_fractionation_specs_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[8,9,10],"printedPages":["16-30","16-31","16-32"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"purity-vs-recovery-denominators","s":"PetroSkills / 16-30, 16-31, 16-32"},{"id":"nf-ngl-07","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"In the textbook example, 40 mol/h of propane enters, 99% must be recovered overhead, and overhead purity is 95 mol% propane. What is the total overhead flow?","a":"About 41.68 mol/h: 40 x 0.99 / 0.95.","w":["39.60 mol/h, because total overhead equals propane overhead.","38.00 mol/h: 40 x 0.95.","99.00 mol/h, because the recovery is 99%."],"e":"First recover 39.6 mol/h of propane, then divide by its 0.95 product fraction. These are the source example conditions, not an operating setpoint or current plant material balance. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-32, 16-33. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_fractionation_specs_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[10,11],"printedPages":["16-32","16-33"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"source-balance-recovery-and-purity","s":"PetroSkills / 16-32, 16-33"},{"id":"nf-ngl-08","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"At a fixed feed composition and rate, why can independently changing distillate draw upset a previously satisfied pair of purity/recovery specifications?","a":"The two specifications constrain the component balance and the resulting product flow rates.","w":["The column can create extra feed component to preserve both specifications.","Distillate draw has no relation to either product composition.","Changing the level controller deletes the material balance."],"e":"The source uses the example balance to explain that arbitrary product-rate changes cannot preserve every specified composition and recovery at the same feed. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-33. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_fractionation_specs_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[11],"printedPages":["16-33"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"two-specs-constrain-material-balance","s":"PetroSkills / 16-33"},{"id":"nf-ngl-09","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"Why can tower-top pressure be somewhat higher than reflux-accumulator pressure in the example condenser system?","a":"Flow experiences pressure loss through the connecting piping and condenser.","w":["The two readings must always be exactly equal.","The accumulator always compresses the vapor after it leaves the condenser.","The difference proves that one instrument is necessarily defective."],"e":"The text distinguishes the two pressure locations and allows for line/condenser loss. Its illustrative differential is not a Clear Fork alarm or fixed offset. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-35. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_condenser_control_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[13],"printedPages":["16-35"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"tower-vs-accumulator-pressure-loss","s":"PetroSkills / 16-35"},{"id":"nf-ngl-10","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"When using an upper-tray temperature as a proxy for overhead composition, which condition does the source explicitly hold constant?","a":"Column pressure.","w":["Every individual liquid molecule stays on the same tray.","Reflux must be zero.","The condenser must be bypassed."],"e":"The stated relationship between temperature and composition is conditional on constant pressure. A pressure change can invalidate a temperature-only composition interpretation. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-40. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_fractionation_reflux_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[19],"printedPages":["16-40"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"temperature-composition-proxy-pressure-condition","s":"PetroSkills / 16-40"},{"id":"nf-ngl-11","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"What response does the source describe after more cool reflux increases liquid traffic toward the bottom?","a":"Bottom temperature can initially fall, after which the temperature controller increases heat input to restore its target.","w":["The reboiler instantly supplies less heat while preserving every temperature.","The bottom inventory stops interacting with upper-column reflux.","More reflux cannot affect the bottom of the tower."],"e":"The text separates the initial cooling/load effect from the subsequent controlled response. It does not supply response times or controller tuning for Clear Fork. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-40. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_fractionation_reflux_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[19],"printedPages":["16-40"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"reflux-bottoms-temperature-controller-response","s":"PetroSkills / 16-40"},{"id":"nf-ngl-12","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"Why can a hot-vapor bypass to a reflux accumulator disturb level as well as pressure?","a":"Bypass vapor warms the surface liquid and promotes vaporization, changing the liquid inventory.","w":["The bypass is only an instrument signal and carries no material.","It always adds cold liquid without changing equilibrium.","It operates the pump motor directly."],"e":"The source describes warming of the condensed liquid and potential unstable level/reflux effects. It is a specific textbook arrangement, not a new installed bypass or operating instruction. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-37. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_condenser_control_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[15],"printedPages":["16-37"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"hot-vapor-bypass-pressure-level-coupling","s":"PetroSkills / 16-37"},{"id":"nf-ngl-13","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"In the source flooded-condenser arrangement, why does a product valve serve pressure control instead of conventional accumulator-level control?","a":"The accumulator is liquid-full; the controlled liquid/vapor interface is in the condenser, changing its effective condensing area.","w":["A full accumulator has no pressure.","A pressure controller can only manipulate a gas valve.","The condenser must be empty for condensation to occur."],"e":"The text explicitly locates the interface in the flooded condenser and describes pressure control through liquid withdrawal. This is not a claim about all reflux drums. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-38. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_condenser_control_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[16],"printedPages":["16-38"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"flooded-condenser-product-pc-role","s":"PetroSkills / 16-38"},{"id":"nf-ngl-14","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"In the illustrated refrigerant-cooled partial condenser, how are tower pressure and the amount condensed for reflux distinguished?","a":"Vapor-product withdrawal controls tower pressure, while refrigerant-side conditions regulate condensation/reflux.","w":["The vapor-product valve and refrigerant valve must always have identical travel.","Tower pressure is controlled only by bottom-product level.","A partial condenser has no liquid reflux."],"e":"The source separates vapor-product pressure control from refrigerant-side condensation duty. The drawing is a generic control example, not a modification to Clear Fork refrigeration. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-38, 16-39. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_condenser_control_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[16,17],"printedPages":["16-38","16-39"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"partial-condenser-separate-pressure-and-reflux","s":"PetroSkills / 16-38, 16-39"},{"id":"nf-ngl-15","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"Why should meeting several listed composition checks not be treated as proof that every product specification is satisfied?","a":"A separate vapor-pressure requirement may still be violated by that composition.","w":["Composition and vapor pressure are unrelated for every mixture.","A specification table makes all other requirements optional.","Vapor pressure can only be checked on pure water."],"e":"The source gives a propane-product example that meets its listed composition checks but has an unacceptable vapor-pressure result. The example is not a current sales contract. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-47. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_fractionation_bottoms_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[26],"printedPages":["16-47"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"composition-vs-vapor-pressure-spec","s":"PetroSkills / 16-47"},{"id":"nf-ngl-16","c":"Fractionation / operating principles","q":"What lesson does the source draw from excess methane entering a downstream deethanizer?","a":"A downstream column cannot be assumed to correct every upstream light-end problem while also satisfying its own product constraints.","w":["Any column can remove all contaminants without affecting another specification.","Methane disappears when the feed tag changes.","Only the bottom level decides all product qualities."],"e":"The text describes a constraint interaction across the fractionation train, including the ethane-product vapor-pressure requirement. It is not a universal instruction to change a specific plant setpoint. Source: PetroSkills, printed 16-47. Textbook teaching; not an installed limit, approved operating procedure or instruction to change live equipment.","refKey":"ps_fractionation_bottoms_12kh","sourceId":"CF_SOURCE_12KH","sourceFile":"2026-07-29 17-23(4).pdf","sourceSHA256":"c8fa677a96a07d39dbb02d222f017135fafe20e9119541c216b93b2a06d4ba30","pdfPages":[26],"printedPages":["16-47"],"release":"12NF","topic":"ngl","learningObjective":"upstream-light-contamination-downstream-limits","s":"PetroSkills / 16-47"}]}}};
